# Caddy config for the SPLIT DEPLOYMENT's Hetzner VM
# (announce.aztec.network).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS NOT `main`'s Caddyfile — do not copy that file's structure here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `main`'s Caddyfile fronts a VM that serves the PUBLIC SITE (Next.js, every
# route from one process) and therefore needs the load-bearing /admin 404
# block plus a Tailscale-User-Login header strip to keep the forgeable admin
# identity header from ever reaching the app over the public internet.
#
# NEITHER of those protections is needed on THIS VM, because THIS VM does not
# serve the public site or the admin console at all — Netlify does (see
# feat/netlify-deployment / the split-infrastructure plan). This VM runs only
# Postgres (fronted by nothing — clients speak the Postgres wire protocol
# directly to the published 5432 port, not through Caddy) and
# signal-cli-rest-api. So this Caddy instance has exactly one job: terminate
# TLS for announce.aztec.network and reverse-proxy to the Signal sidecar,
# gated by a shared secret.
#
# Do NOT add a /admin block, a Tailscale-User-Login strip, or any of
# main's public-route list here — there is nothing on this host for them to
# protect, and copying them in gives a false sense that this file guards
# something it does not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACME EMAIL — same reasoning as main's Caddyfile: deliberately not set via a
# global `email` directive here (breaks the config outright, crash-loop, if
# ACME_EMAIL is present-but-empty, which an unset value in compose's `.env`
# produces). Caddy issues certs fine without an account email.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SHARED-SECRET GATE
#
# signal-cli-rest-api has no authentication of its own. On `main`'s
# deployment that's fine — the sidecar is unreachable from outside the
# private compose network. On THIS deployment it is reverse-proxied through
# a Caddy instance whose 443 is open to the public internet (no tailnet —
# see infra/terraform/vm.tf), so something has to stand in for the network
# isolation `main` relied on. That something is this header check.
#
# The header name and the client that sends it live in
# src/core/signal-auth.ts (SIGNAL_AUTH_HEADER = 'x-announce-signal-secret',
# built by signalAuthHeaders()) — a Caddyfile is plain text and cannot
# import that TS constant, so this is a manual cross-reference: if the
# header name ever changes in signal-auth.ts, it MUST change here too, or
# the gate silently stops matching and every request gets refused (fails
# closed, so at least it's a loud break, not a leak — but still update both
# together).
#
# FAIL-CLOSED REQUIREMENT: this must behave like tickSecretMatches() in
# src/core/tick-auth.ts — an unset/empty configured secret refuses EVERY
# request, never allows them. Caddy has no built-in "compare header to env
# var" primitive, so the gate is a `header` matcher compared against
# {$ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET}.
#
# CAUGHT LOCALLY, NOT OBVIOUS FROM READING THE DOCS: a `header` matcher's
# argument cannot be EMPTY — `header x-announce-signal-secret {$VAR}` with
# $VAR expanding to an empty string produces a directive with a MISSING
# value, and Caddy refuses to even START ("malformed header matcher:
# expected both field and value"), crash-looping the container instead of
# serving a clean refusal. That is a WORSE failure than "closed": the naive
# `{$ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET}` form (no default) looked correct on paper —
# "no header matches an empty string, so every request 403s" — right up
# until the config failed to load at all. Verified locally: the naive
# form crash-looped Caddy the moment ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET was empty.
#
# UPDATE: docker-compose.split.yml's `caddy.environment` now
# sets `ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET: ${ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET:?...}` — a hard
# guard, matching every other secret in that file (POSTGRES_PASSWORD,
# SIGNAL_ACCOUNT, BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY, ANNOUNCE_DOMAIN). An unset
# ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET now refuses to bring `caddy` up AT ALL — compose
# never even reaches the point of starting this container with an empty
# value, so the "present-but-empty-string" case this section originally
# spent most of its length defending against can no longer occur via the
# normal `docker compose up` path this deployment actually uses.
#
# That REMOVES the sentinel's role as the thing standing between an
# operator's mistake and a working (if inert) proxy — an unset secret is
# now a hard stack-up failure, not a silently-inert 403-everything proxy.
# The `{$ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET:...}` default below is kept anyway, as
# genuine belt-and-braces for the one case `:?` in the compose file
# cannot cover: this Caddyfile invoked directly (`caddy run` / `caddy
# validate`) outside docker-compose.split.yml entirely, where the
# variable may be truly unset rather than compose-guarded. In that
# narrower case, Caddy's own `{$VAR:default}` placeholder DOES correctly
# substitute (it only fails to help against a present-but-empty string,
# which `:?` upstream now prevents from ever reaching here) — verified
# directly: `caddy validate` against this exact file with
# ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET genuinely unset in the environment succeeds and
# still fails every request closed via the sentinel mismatch, same as
# before.
#
# The sentinel itself is unchanged: a long, structurally-implausible
# value no real client's header will ever equal (a real client either
# sends the actual configured secret, or sends no header at all per
# signalAuthHeaders() in src/core/signal-auth.ts) — so this still fails
# closed exactly like tickSecretMatches()'s
# `if (!configured || !presented) return false`, just expressed as
# "compare against a value nothing will ever send" instead of "refuse to
# compare at all" (Caddyfile syntax has no boolean short-circuit to
# express the latter directly).
#
# @authorized matches only when the incoming header equals that value (or
# the real secret, once set); handle @authorized reverse-proxies; the
# catch-all handle{} below 403s everything else — missing header, wrong
# value, AND the standalone-Caddyfile sentinel-mismatch case, all the
# same path.
#
# The domain is supplied by the environment, never hardcoded. Compose passes
# ANNOUNCE_DOMAIN through to this container; Caddy obtains and renews TLS for
# it automatically. Renewal and Postgres's cert-reload hook are handled
# entirely outside this file — see infra/ansible/roles/cert_reload.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACME CHALLENGE TYPE. Nothing about this file previously
# disabled HTTP-01, so if TLS-ALPN-01 ever failed for any reason, Caddy
# would fall back to attempting an HTTP-01 challenge on port 80 — a port
# infra/terraform/vm.tf's firewall does NOT open (only 443, 5432, and 22
# are open there; 80 is closed, deliberately, per that file's comment).
# The result would not be a clean, visible error: it would be Caddy
# quietly retrying a challenge type that can never succeed on this host,
# indistinguishable at a glance from ordinary ACME retry/backoff
# behaviour. The `tls { issuer acme { disable_http_challenge } }` block
# below makes the intent explicit: only TLS-ALPN-01 (needs just 443,
# already open) is ever attempted, so a failure there surfaces as a real
# ACME error immediately instead of a silent, unwinnable HTTP-01 retry
# loop.
{$ANNOUNCE_DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		issuer acme {
			disable_http_challenge
		}
	}

	encode zstd gzip

	@authorized header x-announce-signal-secret {$ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET:__announce_signal_secret_unset__}

	handle @authorized {
		reverse_proxy signal:8080
	}

	# Catch-all: anything that didn't match @authorized above — missing
	# header, wrong value, or ANNOUNCE_SIGNAL_SECRET unset. Kept LAST and
	# unconditional so there is no path through this Caddyfile that reaches
	# the sidecar without the check above.
	handle {
		error 403
	}

	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		-Server
	}

	log {
		output stderr
		format console
	}
}
